{
 "fps": 30,
 "lead": 0.8,
 "tail": 2.0,
 "footer": "Sources: canada.ca \u00b7 OAS recovery tax & payment amounts (2026), CRA pension income splitting (T1032), TFSA, OAS deferral \u00b7 Examples, general info, not advice",
 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "file": "vo/L1.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Frank and Linda are retired. Together, their income this year is one hundred and sixty thousand dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2",
   "file": "vo/L2.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Their neighbours, Raj and Mei, have exactly the same. One hundred and sixty thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "L3",
   "file": "vo/L3.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Same income. Same age. Same Old Age Security."
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "file": "vo/L4.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But starting next July, Frank's O A S cheques will shrink. Three thousand, seven hundred and two dollars, gone over twelve months. Raj and Mei lose nothing."
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "file": "vo/L5.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "By the end of this video, you'll know why, and four legal ways to keep more of yours. One of them doesn't cost a single dollar."
  },
  {
   "id": "L6",
   "file": "vo/L6.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "All numbers: twenty twenty-six income, each spouse files a separate return. Examples, not real people. General information, not advice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L7",
   "file": "vo/L7.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "First: what O A S actually is."
  },
  {
   "id": "L8",
   "file": "vo/L8.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Old Age Security is a monthly federal pension for people sixty-five and older. It's separate from the C P P."
  },
  {
   "id": "L9",
   "file": "vo/L9.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "From October to December twenty twenty-six, the maximum is seven hundred and sixty-two dollars and fifty cents a month between sixty-five and seventy-four. From seventy-five, it's about ten percent more."
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "file": "vo/L10.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's roughly nine thousand dollars a year. And it counts as taxable income."
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "file": "vo/L11.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But there's a catch with a long official name: the O A S pension recovery tax. Most people call it the clawback."
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "file": "vo/L12.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, how the clawback works."
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "file": "vo/L13.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For twenty twenty-six income, the line is ninety-five thousand, three hundred and twenty-three dollars of net income."
  },
  {
   "id": "L14",
   "file": "vo/L14.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Every dollar above that line costs you fifteen cents of O A S."
  },
  {
   "id": "L15",
   "file": "vo/L15.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And it's your own net world income. Each spouse is measured separately. Not the household."
  },
  {
   "id": "L16",
   "file": "vo/L16.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's the whole story of Frank and Linda. Frank's net income is one hundred and twenty thousand. Linda's is forty thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "L17",
   "file": "vo/L17.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Raj and Mei have eighty thousand each. Same total, split evenly."
  },
  {
   "id": "L18",
   "file": "vo/L18.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Frank is twenty-four thousand, six hundred and seventy-seven dollars over the line. Fifteen percent of that is three thousand, seven hundred and two dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L19",
   "file": "vo/L19.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Raj and Mei are both under the line. Their clawback is zero."
  },
  {
   "id": "L20",
   "file": "vo/L20.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And here's the timing most people miss. The clawback on twenty twenty-six income isn't taken this year. It comes out of your O A S payments from July twenty twenty-seven to June twenty twenty-eight."
  },
  {
   "id": "L21",
   "file": "vo/L21.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For Frank, that's about three hundred and eight dollars less every month. Almost five months of O A S, spread over a year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L22",
   "file": "vo/L22.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And there's a ceiling. At a net income of about one hundred and fifty-five thousand, three hundred and twenty dollars, the whole O A S is gone. From seventy-five, the ceiling is about one hundred and sixty-one thousand, three hundred and twenty."
  },
  {
   "id": "L23",
   "file": "vo/L23.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The government publishes these limits every year, so check the current ones."
  },
  {
   "id": "L24",
   "file": "vo/L24.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So, how do you keep more? Lever one: pension income splitting."
  },
  {
   "id": "L25",
   "file": "vo/L25.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "From sixty-five, you can move up to half of your eligible pension income to your spouse, on paper. Company pension payments qualify, and from sixty-five, so do R R I F payments."
  },
  {
   "id": "L26",
   "file": "vo/L26.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "You both sign a joint election, form T ten thirty-two, with your returns. No money moves between accounts. Only the tax return changes."
  },
  {
   "id": "L27",
   "file": "vo/L27.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "C P P and O A S themselves can't be split this way."
  },
  {
   "id": "L28",
   "file": "vo/L28.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Say Frank has sixty thousand dollars of company pension and R R I F income. He can move up to thirty thousand to Linda."
  },
  {
   "id": "L29",
   "file": "vo/L29.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "If he moves twenty-five thousand, his net income drops to ninety-five thousand. Linda's rises to sixty-five thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "L30",
   "file": "vo/L30.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Frank is now under the line. His clawback goes from three thousand, seven hundred and two dollars to zero."
  },
  {
   "id": "L31",
   "file": "vo/L31.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Splitting can lower the couple's income tax as well, depending on both incomes. And it's free. That's the move that doesn't cost a single dollar."
  },
  {
   "id": "L32",
   "file": "vo/L32.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "But here's what surprises people. It's not only how much you have. It's where the money sits."
  },
  {
   "id": "L33",
   "file": "vo/L33.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Lever two: the T F S A. Say Frank also has two hundred thousand dollars in G I Cs in a regular account, earning four percent. That's eight thousand dollars of interest a year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L34",
   "file": "vo/L34.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That interest is income. At fifteen percent, it adds one thousand, two hundred dollars of clawback, on top of the income tax."
  },
  {
   "id": "L35",
   "file": "vo/L35.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Inside a T F S A, the same interest doesn't go on your return at all. The C R A says T F S A income and withdrawals don't affect O A S."
  },
  {
   "id": "L36",
   "file": "vo/L36.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So if you have T F S A room, savings there can protect your O A S, year after year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L37",
   "file": "vo/L37.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Lever three: watch the one-time spikes."
  },
  {
   "id": "L38",
   "file": "vo/L38.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Say Frank takes an extra thirty thousand dollars from his R R I F in one year, for a renovation."
  },
  {
   "id": "L39",
   "file": "vo/L39.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "His net income jumps to one hundred and fifty thousand. The clawback jumps to eight thousand, two hundred and two dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L40",
   "file": "vo/L40.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's four thousand, five hundred more O A S gone, starting the following July."
  },
  {
   "id": "L41",
   "file": "vo/L41.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Large withdrawals and taxable capital gains all land in net income for that year. Plan them for years when you're under the line, or use splitting when the income qualifies."
  },
  {
   "id": "L42",
   "file": "vo/L42.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Lever four: when you start O A S."
  },
  {
   "id": "L43",
   "file": "vo/L43.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "You don't have to start at sixty-five. For every month you wait, it goes up by zero point six percent. At seventy, that's thirty-six percent more, for life."
  },
  {
   "id": "L44",
   "file": "vo/L44.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Maria is sixty-five and still working. With O A S, her net income would be one hundred and thirty thousand dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L45",
   "file": "vo/L45.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The clawback would take five thousand, two hundred and two dollars of her roughly nine thousand a year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L46",
   "file": "vo/L46.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "If she waits until seventy, she skips those mostly clawed-back years, and gets thirty-six percent more for life, when her income is lower."
  },
  {
   "id": "L47",
   "file": "vo/L47.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "It's not right for everyone. With a low income, delaying can cost you the Guaranteed Income Supplement, which you can't receive while you delay O A S."
  },
  {
   "id": "L48",
   "file": "vo/L48.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Where this changes. Every number here is the O A S clawback only. Splitting and withdrawals also change your income tax and some credits. For your own numbers, ask a tax professional."
  },
  {
   "id": "L49",
   "file": "vo/L49.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And there's one more lever, before any of this starts: using the low-income years right after you retire. That's our next video."
  },
  {
   "id": "L50",
   "file": "vo/L50.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here's your checklist."
  },
  {
   "id": "L51",
   "file": "vo/L51.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. Find your net income for the year, and compare it with ninety-five thousand, three hundred and twenty-three."
  },
  {
   "id": "L52",
   "file": "vo/L52.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. If you're a couple over sixty-five, check pension splitting on form T ten thirty-two, every year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L53",
   "file": "vo/L53.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Use your T F S A before a regular account."
  },
  {
   "id": "L54",
   "file": "vo/L54.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. Plan big withdrawals and sales. And choose when to start O A S on purpose, not by default."
  },
  {
   "id": "L55",
   "file": "vo/L55.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So, what did we learn?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L56",
   "file": "vo/L56.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. The clawback is fifteen cents for every dollar above ninety-five thousand, three hundred and twenty-three, per person."
  },
  {
   "id": "L57",
   "file": "vo/L57.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. It's taken the following year, from July to June."
  },
  {
   "id": "L58",
   "file": "vo/L58.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Splitting, the T F S A, timing and the start date can keep more of it, legally."
  },
  {
   "id": "L59",
   "file": "vo/L59.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Next on this channel: the retirement tax window most Canadians miss. The low-income years right after you retire. Subscribe, so you don't miss it."
  }
 ],
